00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:13.666
1-3. What was the Fantasy Daily Doodle series?

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:15.750
Some of you may already know this, 

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.416
But I actually started my online artist career

00:02:18.416 --> 00:02:21.166
With a series called Fantasy Daily Doodles.

00:02:21.166 --> 00:02:22.666
The idea was simple.

00:02:22.666 --> 00:02:25.833
I would draw and upload one piece every single day.

00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:28.750
And those pen drawings always came with a story.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.000
 I’d post a drawing with a short caption,

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.250
And a lot of people would react not just to the artwork,

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.000
But to the story behind it.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.000
And I absolutely loved that.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.083
At first, the drawings were basically just collections of random ideas or concepts. 

00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:46.750
But over time, stories and lore started forming around them.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.000
Worlds started developing.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.750
And eventually, recurring characters started showing up as well.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:54.000
And, by the way,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.416
 the Traveler was actually one of the very first characters I ever came up with.

00:02:58.416 --> 00:03:00.666
He wasn’t like this from the start, though...

00:03:00.666 --> 00:03:03.500
But we’ll talk more about that later.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:07.000
Anyway, somehow, we eventually ended up making animations too.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:12.166
And honestly, I see these animations as an extension of my drawing series.

00:03:12.166 --> 00:03:16.000
A lot of our video ideas actually came directly from those drawings

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.583
I had already made.

00:03:17.583 --> 00:03:19.666
So if you discovered me through YouTube,

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:23.333
I definitely recommend checking out some of my older work too.

00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:25.833
They are fun. And there’s a lot of it.

00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:28.166
I post one every day.

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:31.166
2. How the Channel Started
What made you become an animator?

00:03:31.166 --> 00:03:34.000
I didn’t want to

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.416
Seriously

00:03:35.416 --> 00:03:38.166
I had absolutely no plans to make animations,

00:03:38.166 --> 00:03:40.500
Because I already knew how hard it will be.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.083
But this guy, RealRock,

00:03:42.083 --> 00:03:43.416
Strongly recommended it.

00:03:43.416 --> 00:03:46.666
And eventually we decided to try making them together.

00:03:46.666 --> 00:03:48.333
Even when I first started YouTube,

00:03:48.333 --> 00:03:51.750
I was just uploading timelapse drawing videos as Shorts.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.833
And somehow, yeah...

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:55.666
We ended up here

00:03:56.166 --> 00:03:58.666
3. Ideas &amp; Inspiration
Where do you get your ideas, and how do you come up with them?

00:03:58.666 --> 00:04:02.250
I think this is actually pretty difficult for me to explain logically.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:08.000
At this point, it feels like some kind of mechanism has already formed inside my head.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.416
Even I don’t really experience the process as some kind of organized, step-by-step system.

00:04:13.416 --> 00:04:14.916
So let me put it this way.

00:04:14.916 --> 00:04:19.250
There’s already a little creativity factory built inside my head.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.000
I think every artist probably has one of those.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.833
There’s nothing particularly special about mine.

00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:28.583
The factory reflects my own personality and the way I think.

00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:31.916
And all I really do is throw ingredients into it.

00:04:31.916 --> 00:04:34.166
They can be completely random.

00:04:34.166 --> 00:04:40.000
They could be drawings, Movies, Comics, Random scientific facts, History,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.916
Or even something that happened to me in everyday life.

00:04:42.916 --> 00:04:46.250
I throw those things in, and eventually, something comes out.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.666
Maybe it turns into an idea for a drawing.

00:04:48.666 --> 00:04:50.583
Maybe it becomes a story.

00:04:50.583 --> 00:04:54.916
And there are actually certain patterns in the way my imagination tends to work.

00:04:54.916 --> 00:04:57.083
What if A was B?

00:04:57.083 --> 00:05:00.750
Or... What if A didn't have B?

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.583
A lot of my ideas come from that kind of thinking

00:05:03.583 --> 00:05:08.833
Taking something familiar and putting it into a strange or unexpected context.

00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:12.250
Or sometimes, it’s just about twisting a cliché.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:16.333
I think that might be one of the keys to coming up with fresh ideas,

00:05:16.333 --> 00:05:20.333
Not accepting things as obvious just because they’re supposed to be obvious.

00:05:20.333 --> 00:05:22.250
Why does it have to be this way?

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.500
Why are we assuming that this is normal?

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.000
That habit of constantly questioning things often leads to interesting ideas.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.666
Recently, I’ve been really interested in more manga concepts and metafiction.

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:37.750
But it might take a while before that stuff actually shows up in our animations.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:38.833
For some reason,

00:05:38.833 --> 00:05:42.916
The animation side of my brain still isn’t generating ideas that way.

00:05:42.916 --> 00:05:46.166
Maybe I’m still just wired to think in drawings.

00:05:46.166 --> 00:05:50.750
As for questions about where I get ideas for specific characters...

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.916
I think I’ll save those for later,

00:05:52.916 --> 00:05:56.333
Once I’ve actually introduced more of my characters on YouTube. 

00:05:56.833 --> 00:05:59.333
4. Drawing Skills &amp; How I Draw
4-1. What is your drawing process, and what tools do you use?

00:05:59.333 --> 00:06:02.250
Let me tell you about the pen drawings that I make every day.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.166
First, I come up with an idea.

00:06:04.166 --> 00:06:06.166
Secondly, I sketch it.

00:06:06.166 --> 00:06:07.250
Then I ink it.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.333
That's it.

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:10.666
I use a mechanical pencil for sketching,

00:06:10.666 --> 00:06:12.750
And for the actual drawing,

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:15.250
I use Dong-A Fine Tech pens.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:21.583
I usually use 0.25, 0.3, and 0.5 millimeter pens.

00:06:21.583 --> 00:06:25.416
Fine Tech pens are pretty cheap, but the ink flows nicely, so I use them.

00:06:25.416 --> 00:06:28.250
And I use a croquis book for basically the same reason.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:29.166
It’s cheap.

00:06:29.166 --> 00:06:32.333
Although, to be fair, the paper is 105 grams,

00:06:32.333 --> 00:06:34.250
So it’s not that cheap or flimsy.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:37.500
I just like getting good value for my money, okay?

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:38.750
Now, in my Shorts,

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:43.750
You might have seen me drawing directly with a pen without sketching first.

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:45.083
And you might be wondering,

00:06:45.083 --> 00:06:48.416
“Wait, do you actually draw everything without sketches?”

00:06:48.416 --> 00:06:50.500
No. Absolutely not.

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.166
Normally, I rely on sketches all the time.

00:06:53.166 --> 00:06:56.416
I just deliberately don’t sketch when I’m filming.

00:06:56.416 --> 00:06:59.833
Because it looks cooler that way, doesn’t it?

00:06:59.833 --> 00:07:01.833
4-2. How should you practice drawing? How do you get better?

00:07:01.833 --> 00:07:04.583
I  could just say, “Draw a lot,” and end it there.

00:07:04.583 --> 00:07:06.500
It sounds nice and profound.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:08.916
But honestly, that’s the hardest part.

00:07:08.916 --> 00:07:13.250
Learning fundamentals is usually the least fun part of learning how to draw.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:14.750
And at the end of the day,

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:19.000
If drawing itself isn’t fun, it’s really hard to stay motivated.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.166
Personally, I already had a decent foundation.

00:07:22.166 --> 00:07:25.166
I spent about two years studying at an art academy,

00:07:25.166 --> 00:07:27.250
So I learned a lot of the basics there.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:31.500
Basically, I had external pressure forcing me through the most boring part.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:35.333
I’m not necessarily saying everyone should go to an academy, though.

00:07:35.333 --> 00:07:38.666
I personally think learning the fundamentals is important

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:40.666
If you want to work in commercial art.

00:07:40.666 --> 00:07:44.500
But at the same time, I also think that sometimes

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:48.333
But at the same time, I also think that sometimes

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:52.583
Especially if you look at it from a more contemporary art perspective.

00:07:52.583 --> 00:07:54.666
I’ve occasionally seen work by students

00:07:54.666 --> 00:07:58.333
Who never really focused on learning traditional traditional artistic techniques, 

00:07:58.333 --> 00:08:02.750
But instead studied things like philosophy or aesthetics.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.000
And honestly, some of that work

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.416
Was really interesting.

00:08:06.416 --> 00:08:08.083
But anyway, back to the point.

00:08:08.083 --> 00:08:13.666
The method I used was actually the same idea behind starting my Fantasy Daily Doodle series.

00:08:13.666 --> 00:08:16.583
Instead of practicing for the sake of practicing,

00:08:16.583 --> 00:08:18.500
Just start making finished work.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:21.000
That series originally started because

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:25.166
I wanted to turn the random little doodles I used to draw in the corners of my paper

00:08:25.166 --> 00:08:27.333
Into actual finished pieces.

00:08:27.333 --> 00:08:29.333
So I would start with a character idea,

00:08:29.333 --> 00:08:30.833
Or a creature idea,

00:08:30.833 --> 00:08:32.333
Or an idea for a world,

00:08:32.333 --> 00:08:33.416
Or a story.

00:08:33.416 --> 00:08:35.750
And then I would turn it into something.

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.916
I repeated that process every single day.

00:08:38.916 --> 00:08:42.666
So I was getting the enjoyment of actually creating finished work,

00:08:42.666 --> 00:08:45.333
While also improving through repetition.

00:08:45.333 --> 00:08:46.583
And maybe you're thinking,

00:08:46.583 --> 00:08:50.500
“But I’m not good enough yet. I don’t even like the finished pieces I make.”

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:51.500
That's okay.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:55.416
Someday, you might actually look back and find something charming about them.

00:08:55.416 --> 00:09:01.333
Personally, I still miss some of the work I made when I was much rougher as an artist.

00:09:01.333 --> 00:09:03.333
4-3. How did you develop your current style, and what influenced it?

00:09:03.333 --> 00:09:06.666
I think my style was heavily influenced by comics.

00:09:06.666 --> 00:09:10.333
Especially the comics and illustrated books I grew up with.

00:09:10.333 --> 00:09:16.500
For example, I think Terry Deary's Horrible Histories series had a pretty big influence on me.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.083
Things like the sense of humor, the visual rendering of characters,

00:09:20.083 --> 00:09:23.500
 and the overall way the illustrations were presented.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.916
And the format of my Fantasy Daily Doodle series

00:09:25.916 --> 00:09:29.666
Was also heavily influenced by The Far Side Gallery.

00:09:29.666 --> 00:09:34.000
There was also another illustrated book that had a huge impact on me.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.750
I randomly read it in a hospital years ago, and it completely blew my mind.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:43.583
Each page showed some kind of mysterious event happening in everyday life.

00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:46.916
There was usually only a sentence or so of text.

00:09:46.916 --> 00:09:49.500
But it didn’t really explain what was happening.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.750
And I loved that mysterious feeling.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:54.250
That was also the big influence on my series.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:55.666
The problem is...

00:09:55.666 --> 00:09:58.833
I have absolutely no idea what the book was called.

00:09:58.833 --> 00:10:02.000
So if anyone knows what I’m talking about, please let me know.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.250
All I remember is that it had this bluish monochrome look, 

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:08.500
With rough, almost pencil-like shading, 

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:12.500
And relatively realistic drawings without much stylization.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.416
Honestly, though, I think the biggest influences on my work

00:10:15.416 --> 00:10:19.833
Also came from the manga and animations I encountered when I was younger.

00:10:19.833 --> 00:10:22.916
After that, I stopped reading manga for quite a long time.

00:10:22.916 --> 00:10:26.500
I’ve only recently started getting back into them to study.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.333
I’ve also gotten a lot of inspiration from Ian McQue’s drawings.

00:10:30.333 --> 00:10:34.583
Looking at his work taught me how to use lines in a much freer way.

00:10:34.583 --> 00:10:36.833
Aaron Horkey’s illustrations

00:10:36.833 --> 00:10:41.500
Helped me a lot with developing more control and skill in my linework.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:44.083
The same goes for Franklin Booth.

00:10:44.083 --> 00:10:46.916
And back when I was using watercolors more often,

00:10:46.916 --> 00:10:51.333
I also looked at Arthur Rackham’s illustrations for a lot of inspiration.

00:10:51.333 --> 00:10:53.333
4-4. What keeps you motivated to continue?

00:10:53.333 --> 00:10:57.666
I think getting immediate feedback and reaction also played a huge role.

00:10:57.666 --> 00:11:00.833
Whenever I posted a drawing or uploaded a video,

00:11:00.833 --> 00:11:03.833
People would react to it almost immediately.

00:11:03.833 --> 00:11:07.666
Of course, I enjoy drawing enough to do it every day without stopping.

00:11:07.666 --> 00:11:08.666
But honestly,

00:11:08.666 --> 00:11:11.333
If I couldn’t feel or see people’s reactions

00:11:11.333 --> 00:11:14.500
I don’t think I would have enjoyed it nearly as much.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:20.166
And I think the storytelling aspect also helped create stronger reactions from people.

00:11:20.166 --> 00:11:24.750
When you add an interesting story, concept, or creative idea,

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:28.166
People don’t just look at the artwork and go, “Oh, that’s cool.”

00:11:28.166 --> 00:11:31.666
A lot of them actually start immersing themselves in it

00:11:31.666 --> 00:11:34.833
And continuing the story in their own imagination.

00:11:34.833 --> 00:11:36.750
And that...

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:39.333
That was genuinely a lot of fun.

00:11:39.833 --> 00:11:42.333
5. Animation Production
5-1. What does the animation process look like?

00:11:42.333 --> 00:11:45.250
Let me briefly explain how we make our animations.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:49.000
First, I come up with the idea and put together a rough storyboard.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.916
Once we’ve gone over that, we start working.

00:11:51.916 --> 00:11:55.166
I handle all of the artwork and prepare everything for animation

00:11:55.166 --> 00:11:57.916
By separating the different elements into layers.

00:11:57.916 --> 00:12:00.333
Then RealRock animates them.

00:12:00.333 --> 00:12:05.333
Once he finishes a scene, we review it, and then move on to the next one.

00:12:05.333 --> 00:12:07.166
After all the animation is finished,

00:12:07.166 --> 00:12:10.750
We add the sound, and eventually the video is complete.

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:13.916
The exact process can change depending on the project though,

00:12:13.916 --> 00:12:18.333
So I think that’s about as much detail as we need to get into for now.

00:12:18.333 --> 00:12:20.333
5-2. How long does it take to make one animation?

00:12:20.333 --> 00:12:24.166
  The production time depends on the difficulty of the project,

00:12:24.166 --> 00:12:27.500
The idea itself, and the overall length of the video.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:30.250
Some projects have taken around three or four weeks,

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:33.000
While others have taken two or three months.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:37.083
And unfortunately, the time it takes me to prepare the artwork and assets

00:12:37.083 --> 00:12:39.666
And the time it takes RealRock to edit and animate

00:12:39.666 --> 00:12:41.083
Aren't that different.

00:12:41.083 --> 00:12:43.833
So there’s only so much we can shorten.

00:12:43.833 --> 00:12:46.416
That said, when I prepare the assets,

00:12:46.416 --> 00:12:50.916
I try to make sure RealRock doesn’t have to deal with unnecessary extra work

00:12:50.916 --> 00:12:52.333
During the animation process.

00:12:52.333 --> 00:12:54.166
And on the editing side,

00:12:54.166 --> 00:12:59.833
We use various plugins and expressions to make the workflow as efficient as possible.

00:12:59.833 --> 00:13:01.833
5-3. What tools do you use?

00:13:01.833 --> 00:13:03.833
For the artwork and asset creation,

00:13:03.833 --> 00:13:07.083
I use Photoshop and draw with an Intuos Pro tablet.

00:13:07.083 --> 00:13:09.833
Then we bring everything into After Effects

00:13:09.833 --> 00:13:13.083
and Premiere Pro to turn it into an actual video.

00:13:13.083 --> 00:13:17.583
And someone also asked which part of the production process is the most stressful.

00:13:17.583 --> 00:13:20.166
Well... Seriously,

00:13:20.166 --> 00:13:21.083
What an absolutely

00:13:21.083 --> 00:13:21.833
Awfu.. prog..

00:13:21.833 --> 00:13:23.833
What a wonderful program!

00:13:23.833 --> 00:13:26.833
6. Worldbuilding &amp; Lore

00:13:26.833 --> 00:13:28.250
Hmm.....

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:30.083
Should I really talk about this?

00:13:30.083 --> 00:13:32.916
I feel like this could turn into a spoiler.

00:13:32.916 --> 00:13:36.750
But if I explain it in the same vague way I usually approach my work...

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:38.750
A lot of the stories are connected.

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:41.416
But some of them exist completely on their own.

00:13:41.416 --> 00:13:44.583
We’re also planning to start releasing series on YouTube

00:13:44.583 --> 00:13:46.416
That feature recurring characters,

00:13:46.416 --> 00:13:49.250
So I think the connections between some of the stories

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:52.000
Might become easier to understand over time.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.666
I know you guys are curious about this stuff, but...

00:13:54.666 --> 00:13:56.666
You’ll just have to wait and see.

00:13:56.666 --> 00:13:59.666
7. Characters

00:13:59.666 --> 00:14:02.833
I think I’m going to save most of these questions for later too.

00:14:02.833 --> 00:14:06.750
There are still a lot of interesting characters I haven’t introduced yet,

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.500
And there’ll be more of them coming in the future.

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:10.583
So...

00:14:10.583 --> 00:14:12.666
Let’s talk about them another time.

00:14:12.666 --> 00:14:15.666
8. Older Works &amp; Possible Sequels

00:14:15.666 --> 00:14:20.000
A lot of our current video ideas actually come from the Fantasy Daily Doodle series,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:24.500
So I think there’s definitely a lot you can look forward to in the long run.

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:25.500
At the moment,

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:29.250
We tend to prioritize whichever animation ideas and storyboards

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:31.916
Feel the most interesting or exciting to us.

00:14:31.916 --> 00:14:32.833
Although..

00:14:32.833 --> 00:14:36.166
It would be really nice if we had more people working with us.

00:14:36.166 --> 00:14:39.166
9. Plans for Longer Videos &amp; Series

00:14:39.166 --> 00:14:40.250
Well...

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:42.000
That would be a spoiler too.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:43.666
So I’m keeping my mouth shut.

00:14:44.166 --> 00:14:46.666
10. Activities Outside of the Channel &amp; Future Plans

00:14:46.666 --> 00:14:48.666
I actually feel pretty bad about this,

00:14:48.666 --> 00:14:52.666
Especially toward the people who have been following my work even before YouTube.

00:14:52.666 --> 00:14:55.500
Because honestly, there are a lot of things I wanted to do.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:59.000
Exhibitions, selling artwork, publishing books...

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:01.833
There are a lot of things I’d love to try.

00:15:01.833 --> 00:15:02.916
And in some ways,

00:15:02.916 --> 00:15:06.833
I also feel like I owe it to the people who have been supporting my work.

00:15:06.833 --> 00:15:08.250
The problem is...

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:10.250
I have terrible stamina.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:14.250
And I’m also the kind of artist who has a very narrow mental bandwidth.

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:18.083
I’m not very good at juggling multiple ideas and plans at the same time.

00:15:18.083 --> 00:15:22.166
So trying to do all of those things while continuing to make my current work

00:15:22.166 --> 00:15:24.083
Has been really difficult.

00:15:24.083 --> 00:15:26.833
This might sound incredibly shameless, but...

00:15:26.833 --> 00:15:30.166
Are there any publishing company CEOs watching this..?

00:15:30.666 --> 00:15:33.166
11. Some Personal Things You Guys Were Curious About

00:15:33.166 --> 00:15:36.333
When it comes to movies, games, and other kinds of media,

00:15:36.333 --> 00:15:39.916
I generally tend to go for things that are well-reviewed.

00:15:39.916 --> 00:15:44.000
Basically, before I think about fandom or personal preference,

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.916
I tend to care more about how well something is actually made.

00:15:47.916 --> 00:15:52.333
I’m the kind of person who gets excited when I can look at something and think,

00:15:52.333 --> 00:15:56.083
“Wow. They really nailed what they were trying to do here.”

00:15:56.083 --> 00:15:57.083
So yeah.

00:15:57.083 --> 00:15:59.916
I guess I’m kind of a critic-type consumer.

00:15:59.916 --> 00:16:02.500
Lately, I’ve been playing a lot more games.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:05.583
I especially like exploration-focused games.

00:16:05.583 --> 00:16:08.416
The kind that don’t explain everything to you,

00:16:08.416 --> 00:16:10.666
Where you get that sense of the mysteries,

00:16:10.666 --> 00:16:13.916
And where you can really explore a rich and detailed world.

00:16:13.916 --> 00:16:18.250
The Hollow Knight series, Skyrim, The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild,

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:23.333
And Disco Elysium would probably be some of my all-time favorite games at the moment.

00:16:23.333 --> 00:16:25.916
These days, I’ve been checking out highly rated games

00:16:25.916 --> 00:16:28.416
And trying out all kinds of different genres.

00:16:28.416 --> 00:16:31.083
For movies, my all-time favorite always changes.

00:16:31.083 --> 00:16:32.750
For now, I can think of…

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:36.166
The Green Knight, The Irishman, The Master.

00:16:36.166 --> 00:16:38.333
But getting back to my taste in general...

00:16:38.333 --> 00:16:40.583
I’m actually a pretty annoying consumer.

00:16:40.583 --> 00:16:43.250
I like works that are good at pretty much everything.

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:45.250
But just being good at everything

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:48.833
Isn’t enough for something to become one of my all-time favorites.

00:16:48.833 --> 00:16:50.916
It needs to be excellent across the board,

00:16:50.916 --> 00:16:55.000
AND it needs at least one area where it goes completely insane.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.416
You know, that one element where the creator’s personality

00:16:58.416 --> 00:17:01.833
Or artistic vision just goes absolutely crazy.

00:17:01.833 --> 00:17:03.500
That kind of auteur touch.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:08.166
Lately, though, I’ve been putting so much of my life into my own work

00:17:08.166 --> 00:17:13.000
That I haven’t had as much time to enjoy other people’s work as I used to.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:16.833
I used to be a huge reader, but ever since I started drawing...

00:17:16.833 --> 00:17:19.666
I barely read books anymore. Bummer.

00:17:19.666 --> 00:17:20.916
12.

00:17:20.916 --> 00:17:22.666
12. Closing

00:17:22.666 --> 00:17:23.666
So...

00:17:23.666 --> 00:17:25.666
I guess that’s enough for today?

00:17:25.666 --> 00:17:26.916
All right then.

00:17:26.916 --> 00:17:30.083
Hopefully, we’ll get another chance to do this again sometime!

00:17:30.083 --> 00:17:30.833
Now...

00:17:30.833 --> 00:17:33.666
We should probably get started on the next video.

00:17:34.166 --> 00:17:37.666
Thank you for watching!

